---
// Animation: a bolted joint shaken sideways. Below the slip load nothing turns; above it the nut turns back, the
// torque stripe opens and the preload runs away; a dowel stops the slip. State: loose() in src/lib/explainers.ts.
import Explainer from './Explainer.astro';
import { LOOSE, loose, loosePlot, attrs } from '../lib/explainers';

const S = loose([1, 1, 1, 1]);
const { cx, plot } = LOOSE;
const { X, Y } = loosePlot;
const steps = [
  {
    ms: LOOSE.ms[0],
    html: '<b>Shake the joint sideways, below its slip load.</b> Friction at the interface carries the load. Nothing slides, and the nut does not turn.',
  },
  {
    ms: LOOSE.ms[1],
    html: '<b>Shake it above the slip load.</b> The plates slide in each cycle. While they slide, the friction on the thread cannot also hold the nut, and the thread ramp turns it back a little. The torque stripe opens.',
  },
  {
    ms: LOOSE.ms[2],
    html: '<b>The loosening runs away.</b> Less preload gives a lower slip load, so the slip gets larger and the nut turns faster. The preload falls away in a few more cycles.',
  },
  {
    ms: LOOSE.ms[3],
    html: '<b>Stop the slip.</b> A new joint has a dowel to carry the shear. The same shaking gives no slip, and the preload stays. Preload, with no slip, is the first lock.',
  },
];
---

<Explainer name="loose" title="Animation · why a shaken nut turns back" steps={steps}>
  <svg viewBox="0 0 860 320" role="img" aria-labelledby="ln-desc">
    <desc id="ln-desc">
      Left: a bolt clamps two plates, and the top plate is shaken sideways. A torque stripe runs from the nut onto the
      plate. Right: the preload against the number of cycles. The slipping joint loses its preload slowly at first and
      then quickly. A new joint with a dowel keeps all of its preload.
    </desc>
    <defs>
      <marker id="ln-ah" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="5" markerHeight="5" orient="auto-start-reverse">
        <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 Z" fill="var(--rd-ink)"></path>
      </marker>
    </defs>

    <!-- left: the joint -->
    <text x="40" y="30" font-size="13" fill="var(--rd-muted)" letter-spacing="1">JOINT, SHAKEN SIDEWAYS</text>
    <text data-k="stat" x="40" y="50" font-size="13" fill="var(--rd-accent)">{S.stat.text}</text>
    <rect x="60" y="190" width="280" height="40" fill="var(--rd-steel)" fill-opacity="0.5" stroke="var(--rd-steel)"></rect>
    <rect x={cx - 34} y="230" width="68" height="22" fill="var(--rd-ink)" fill-opacity="0.75"></rect>
    <g data-k="top" {...attrs(S.top)}>
      <rect x="60" y="150" width="280" height="40" fill="var(--rd-steel)" fill-opacity="0.6" stroke="var(--rd-steel)"></rect>
      <rect x={cx - 7} y="110" width="14" height="14" fill="var(--rd-steel)"></rect>
      <rect x={cx - 30} y="124" width="60" height="26" fill="var(--rd-ink)" fill-opacity="0.8"></rect>
      <line x1={cx - 10} y1="124" x2={cx - 10} y2="150" stroke="var(--rd-panel)" stroke-opacity="0.5"></line>
      <line x1={cx + 10} y1="124" x2={cx + 10} y2="150" stroke="var(--rd-panel)" stroke-opacity="0.5"></line>
      <line x1={cx + 8} y1="150" x2={cx + 8} y2="166" stroke="var(--rd-warn)" stroke-width="4"></line>
      <line data-k="stripe" y1="126" y2="148" {...attrs(S.stripe)} stroke="var(--rd-warn)" stroke-width="4"></line>
      <text x={cx + 40} y="140" font-size="12" fill="var(--rd-warn)" class="halo">torque stripe</text>
      <text data-k="turnLab" x={cx - 40} y="140" font-size="12" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--rd-accent)" class="halo" {...attrs(S.turnLab)}>nut turns back</text>
      <line x1="350" y1="172" x2="398" y2="172" stroke="var(--rd-ink)" stroke-width="2" marker-start="url(#ln-ah)" marker-end="url(#ln-ah)"></line>
      <text x="374" y="162" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--rd-ink)">shake</text>
    </g>
    <line data-k="shank" y1="150" x2={cx} y2="230" {...attrs(S.shank)} stroke="var(--rd-ink)" stroke-width="3" stroke-dasharray="4 3" opacity="0.7"></line>
    <g data-k="dowel" {...attrs(S.dowel)}>
      <rect x="104" y="156" width="10" height="68" rx="2" fill="var(--rd-ok)" fill-opacity="0.9"></rect>
      <text x="109" y="248" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--rd-ok)">dowel</text>
    </g>
    <text data-k="fLab" x="40" y="300" font-size="13" fill="var(--rd-ink)">{S.fLab.text}</text>
    <text data-k="tLab" x="200" y="300" font-size="13" fill="var(--rd-ink)">{S.tLab.text}</text>

    <line x1="440" y1="12" x2="440" y2="312" stroke="var(--rd-line)"></line>

    <!-- right: preload against cycles -->
    <text x={plot.x0} y="30" font-size="13" fill="var(--rd-muted)" letter-spacing="1">PRELOAD AGAINST CYCLES</text>
    <line x1={plot.x0} y1={plot.y0} x2={plot.x1} y2={plot.y0} stroke="var(--rd-muted)"></line>
    <line x1={plot.x0} y1={plot.y0} x2={plot.x0} y2={plot.y1 - 8} stroke="var(--rd-muted)"></line>
    {[0, 0.5, 1].map((f) => (
      <text x={plot.x0 - 8} y={Y(f) + 4} font-size="12" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--rd-muted)">{`${f * 100} %`}</text>
    ))}
    <text x={plot.x1} y={plot.y0 + 18} font-size="12" text-anchor="end" fill="var(--rd-muted)">cycles</text>
    <line x1={X(LOOSE.start)} y1={plot.y1} x2={X(LOOSE.start)} y2={plot.y0} stroke="var(--rd-muted)" stroke-dasharray="4 4"></line>
    <text x={X(LOOSE.start) + 5} y={plot.y0 - 8} font-size="12" fill="var(--rd-muted)">slip starts</text>
    <polyline data-k="curveB" {...attrs(S.curveB)} fill="none" stroke="var(--rd-ok)" stroke-width="2.5"></polyline>
    <polyline data-k="curveA" {...attrs(S.curveA)} fill="none" stroke="var(--rd-accent)" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linejoin="round"></polyline>
    <line x1="690" y1="112" x2="712" y2="112" stroke="var(--rd-accent)" stroke-width="2.5"></line>
    <text x="718" y="116" font-size="13" fill="var(--rd-accent)">slipping joint</text>
    <g data-k="legB" {...attrs(S.legB)}>
      <line x1="690" y1="132" x2="712" y2="132" stroke="var(--rd-ok)" stroke-width="2.5"></line>
      <text x="718" y="136" font-size="13" fill="var(--rd-ok)">with a dowel</text>
    </g>
  </svg>
  <Fragment slot="caption">
    Schematic: the slip and the rotation are exaggerated, and the cycles are for illustration only. The preload curve
    has the shape of a Junker test.
  </Fragment>
</Explainer>
